WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.663
Peel away the onion, and cells still hug the nerve ending.

2
00:00:07.603 --> 00:00:08.603
What do they add?

3
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:11.953
First, the settled step.

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:17.513
In mice, force opens a channel called Piezo2, in the sensory axon itself.

5
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:21.877
Delete it from the wrapping cells, and touch responses stay.

6
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:25.887
But the wrapping cells feel force too.

7
00:00:26.747 --> 00:00:30.927
In duck bill corpuscles, they carry their own mechanically activated currents.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.260
In duck Grandry corpuscles, the Meissner type, depolarizing one wrapping cell could

9
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.960
make its fibre fire, after a variable delay.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.333
In mice, switching Pacinian wrapping cells on with light drove the fibre.

11
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.830
Switching them off meant a vibration had to be about five times larger to be

12
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:54.830
detected.

13
00:00:58.283 --> 00:01:00.343
So these cells contribute to detection.

14
00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:03.103
How they normally do it is not yet known.

15
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:07.863
A 200 Hz vibration repeats every 5 milliseconds.

16
00:01:08.863 --> 00:01:14.003
In duck Grandry corpuscles, delays ran from 20 milliseconds to about 9 seconds.

17
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:18.563
Predict. Could those cells be passing on each cycle?

18
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:29.217
A steady delay could shift every cycle, so delay alone cannot rule it out.

19
00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:33.077
But the slow, scattered firing suggests a supporting role.

20
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:38.987
Those delays followed electrical depolarization of a wrapping cell, not vibration.

21
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:42.007
Passing each cycle needs a direct test.

22
00:01:42.827 --> 00:01:45.647
For Pacinian wrapping cells, the question is open.

23
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.190
Meissner corpuscles hold a surprise too.

24
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.990
In mice, most hold two molecularly distinct nerve fibres, told apart by genetic

25
00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:55.990
labels.

26
00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:02.070
Mice lacking TrkB in sensory neurons from development grew up without Meissner

27
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:05.990
corpuscles. They needed about twice the push to detect a touch.

28
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.040
They still detected stronger pushes.

29
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.880
So these corpuscles help set sensitivity, but in this experiment detection survived

30
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:15.880
without them.

31
00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:24.273
In freely moving mice, Pacinian neurons fired during the mouse's own movements.

32
00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:30.677
The most sensitive also answered vibrations from a tap on the branch 2.5 metres

33
00:02:30.677 --> 00:02:31.677
away.

34
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:37.427
Two detectors of change, each a nerve ending and the cells that help it feel.
